## ABSTRACT
Photo-induced vacancy defects are employed strategically to imbue semiconductors with enhanced performance characteristics for many important applications such as surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) sensing, photocatalysis, and photovoltaic applications. However, the long-term maintenance and use of photo-induced vacancy defects remain elusive, because of their rapid self-healing upon air exposure. In this study, we demonstrate that photo-induced oxygen vacancy \((\mathsf{P I V}_{0})\) defects can be stabilized by the photoexcitation of metal–organic framework (MOF) materials, which is crucial for SERS analysis. The \(\mathsf{P I V}_{0}\) defects in MOF materials are stable for at least two weeks in the ambient atmosphere, owing to the combination of steric hindrance and electron delocalization around vacancy defects, which significantly contrasts the short lifetime (within minutes) of \(\mathsf{P I V}_{0}\) defects in metal-oxide semiconductors. With the formation of stable \(\mathsf{P I V}_{0}\) defects, a prominent SERS enhancement surpassing that of pristine MOFs is achieved, accompanied with a reduced limit of detection by three orders of magnitude. Moreover, the additional SERS enhancement rendered by \(\mathsf{P I V}_{0}\) defects can be stably retained and is effective for monitoring various small molecules, such as dopamine and bisphenol A.
## KEYWORDS
stable photo-induced vacancy defects, metal–organic framework materials, steric hindrance, electron delocalization, Raman spectroscopy
## 1 Introduction
Vacancy defect engineering in semiconductors is an interesting and important topic from the viewpoint of semiconductor physics and applications such as photocatalysis, photovoltaic, and sensing applications [1–6]. Both theoretical and experimental studies have revealed that vacancy defects affect the optical absorption, separation, and migration of photogenerated carriers, as well as the electrical conductivity and surface properties of semiconductors; hence, they significantly affect the applications of semiconductors [1, 7, 8]. As an example, vacancy defects have revolutionized for semiconductor-based surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) substrates in recent years. It was discovered that the SERS sensitivity of semiconductors can be increased significantly up to \(\sim\,10^{-10^{7}}\) by the introduction of a suitable density of oxygen vacancy defects; this strongly suggests that vacancy defects are critical in improving the SERS activity of semiconductors [9–11]. Fundamentally, two types of vacancy defects are generated from different origins, i.e., native defects and defects initiated by external excitation [12]. Native defects are intrinsically introduced during the synthesis of semiconductors, whereas defects initiated by external excitation can be created because of other external-field-induced effects such as high-energy light irradiation—these defects are termed photo-induced defects. Photo-induced defects, which are associated with exciton–lattice interactions, are an important origin of nonequilibrium states that dynamically exist in defective materials, which typically exhibit unique electronic properties that differ significantly from native defects with fixed electronic states [12]. More importantly, photo-induced vacancy defects are advantageous in terms of their universal applicability to multiple semiconductor materials, which affects the SERS behavior of semiconductors, particularly in view of charge-transfer induced enhancement. However, previ