Photo-induced defects, which are associated with exciton–lattice interactions, are an important origin of nonequilibrium states that dynamically exist in defective materials, which typically exhibit unique electronic properties that differ significantly from native defects with fixed electronic states [12]. More importantly, photoinduced vacancy defects are advantageous in terms of their universal applicability to multiple semiconductor materials, which affects the SERS behavior of semiconductors, particularly in view of charge-transfer induced enhancement. However, previous advances in semiconductor-based SERS rely overwhelmingly upon the introduction of native defects, whereas research pertaining to photo-induced vacancy defects is scarce; this is primarily due to the instability of photo-induced vacancy defects in semiconductors upon air exposure [2, 13, 14], which is definitely detrimental to SERS analysis. Therefore, the identification of effective strategies for addressing stable photoinduced vacancy defects to improve SERS performance is meaningful and of great scientific value.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are an attractive class of porous crystalline materials comprising metal centers coordinated to organic ligands for forming well-organized frameworks [15]. The high surface area, high porosity, structural tunability, and adequate chemical and thermal stabilities of MOFs render them
promising candidates for a wide range of potential applications such as gas storage and separation, liquid separation and purification, electrochemical energy storage, catalysis, and sensing [16–18]. What must be specially stressed here is that MOFs can serve as an emerging material platform for stabilizing photo-induced vacancy defects. MOFs can undergo effective electron–hole separation upon photoexcitation, which enables them to behave like semiconductors, where the photo-induced electrons are trapped and preserved in the metal nodes, resulting in the formation of photo-induced mixed-valence compounds; meanwhile, oxygen vacancy defects may be resulted from the breakage of the bonds between reductive metal sites and oxygen atoms. However, in contrast to semiconductors, the unique structural features of MOFs stabilize the presence of photoinduced vacancy defects in MOFs: (1) The ligand specificity of MOFs can cause a delocalization of the electrons around the vacancy defects, thereby decreasing their electronegativity; (2) The dangling hydroxyl groups in the ligands generated by breaking metal–O bonds can provide protection to the adjacent defect center, based on steric hindrance against the accessory of atmospheric oxygen (Scheme 1). In such a configuration, long-lifetime photo-induced vacancy defects in MOFs can be achieved. The presence of photo-induced defects in MOFs alters the electronic energy states that facilitate charge transfer transitions for the substrate–analyte system, which contributes to the significantly improved SERS activity. In fact, MOFs have ever been used in SERS by employing their porous three-dimensional (3D) network structure as a scaffold for metal particles, with their SERS performance mainly from the metal particles. However, the inherent properties of MOFs as advanced SERS substrates are yet to be disclosed [19, 20].
In this study, we demonstrate that the stabilization of photoinduced oxygen vacancy \(\mathrm{(PIV_{O})}\) defects can be achieved by photoexcitation of MOF materials, which is crucial for SERS analysis. To achieve this aim, MIL- \(125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\), a prototype Ti-based MOF material, was synthesized to demonstrate the extraordinary stability of \(\mathrm{PIV_{O}}\) defects, which remain stable for more than two weeks owing to the combination of steric hindrance and electron delocalization around vacancy defects. By contrast, \(\mathrm{PIV_{O}}\) defects
are unstable in typical semiconductors (e.g., \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)), i.e., they vanish upon air exposure within minutes. As a result of stabilized \(\mathrm{PIV_{O}}\) defects in defective MIL- \(125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\), prominent SERS enhancement surpassing that of the pristine MIL- \(125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) can be achieved, accompanied by a further reduction in the limit of detection (LOD) by three orders of magnitude for the detection of 4-tert-butyl catechol (4-TBC) as a probe molecule. The enhanced SERS activities in defective MIL- \(125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) are available up to 120 days after the generation of \(\mathrm{PIV_{O}}\) defects for the detection of various small molecules, such as dopamine and bisphenol A. In fact, the photo-activated strategy is widely applicable to various types of MOF materials. Our findings indicate that \(\mathrm{PIV_{O}}\) defects in MOFs result in modified energy bands that facilitate both the intervalence charge transfer within MOFs and interfacial charge-transfer transitions, which are crucial to SERS in terms of the chemical enhancement mechanism in the MOF–analyte system.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Synthesis and characterization
annihilation spectrometry has been presented to prove that oxygen-vacancy clusters are the overwhelming defects in the D\(\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) (2 h), as shown in Fig. S2 in the ESM [30–32]. The exceptional stability of the \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects in D-MIL- \(125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) was subsequently confirmed by comparing the XPS and ESR spectra of D-MIL- \(125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) immediately after light irradiation with those of D-MIL- \(125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) up to 2 weeks after light irradiation (Figs. 1(c)–1(e) and Fig. S1 in the ESM). The overall features of the XPS and ESR spectra of D-MIL- \(125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) remained almost unchanged even after several weeks of storage in air, indicating that the \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects in D-MIL- \(125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) can exist stably for a prolonged time. For comparison, the stability of photo-induced vacancy defects in the photo-activated \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and Au-modified \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) was also evaluated via the XPS and ESR spectra (Figs. S3–S5 in the ESM). The characteristic XPS and ESR spectra of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and Au-modified \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) several minutes after illumination were similar to those of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and Au-modified \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) before illumination, i.e., the photo-induced defects vanished rapidly in these samples upon air exposure. Our spectroscopic investigation provides evidence supporting the abundance and preservation of \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects in MOFs, whereas \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects in traditional semiconductors are prone to rapid self-healing.
## 2.2 Stable SERS behaviors enabled by photo-induced oxygen vacancy defects
Dopamine, which functions as a neurotransmitter enable pulse transmission in cells, was used as a prototype probe molecule to evaluate the strongly enhanced SERS activity of MOFs enabled by \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects. In fact, the rapid and sensitive determination of dopamine is vital to the diagnosis of various diseases such as Parkinson's disease [33, 34]. First, comparative experiments were conducted by recording the SERS spectra of dopamine \((6.6\times10^{-4}\;\mathrm{M})\) adsorbed on bare \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Si},\) initial MIL- \(125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) D\(\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) (1 h), D-MIL- \(125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) (2 h), photo-excited \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) particles, and colloidal \(\mathrm{Ag}\) and Au nanoparticles under laser irradiation at \(532\ \mathrm{nm},\) as shown in Fig. 2(a). Whereas no clear signals associated with dopamine molecules were observed on the bare \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Si}\) and Au nanoparticles, and an extremely weak signal was observed in the initial MIL- \(125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2},\) a substantial Raman enhancement was observed in D-MIL- \(125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) (1 h), D-MIL-125-
\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\textrm{h})\) , the \(\mathrm{Ag}\) nanoparticles, and the photo-excited \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) particles. Furthermore, the amplified SERS signals as a function of the illumination time can be clearly discerned in defective MIL\(125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) . The prominent SERS signal at \(1,486~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) obtained from \(\mathrm{D-MIL-125-NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\textrm{h})\) was approximately 11 times that of the pristine \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH}_{2}\) substrate, indicating that the significance of \(\mathrm{PIV}_{0}\) defects toward the SERS performance. Notably, D-MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\mathrm{h})\) yielded a much stronger SERS response than the photoexcited \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) particle (more than five times greater), comparable with that of \(\mathrm{Ag}\) nanoparticles, whereas the extension of light irradiation time beyond \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) significantly diminished or completely terminated the SERS signal probably resulted from the collapse of the cluster-ligand framework topologies (Fig. S6 in the ESM). In particular, the SERS spectra of dopamine on \(\mathrm{D-MIL-125-NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\mathrm{h})\) were dominated by three intense peaks at approximately 645, 1,334, and \(1,486~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , which can be attributed to the \(\mathrm{C-H}\) wagging, asymmetric \(\mathrm{C-H}\) twisting, and phenolic vibrations, respectively [35, 36]. Notably, the \(1,486~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) peak appeared as a new peak when compared with the normal Raman spectra of dopamine powder, which is associated with the chemical bonding of dopamine to surface Ti sites via phenolic groups [36] (Fig. 2(b)). It is noteworthy that the peak positions for 645 and \(1,334~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) shifted considerably compared with those obtained for the pristine dopamine powder. The appearance of new peaks and the shift of the intrinsic peaks support the hypothesis that the SERS signal enhancement of \(\mathrm{D-MIL-125-NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\mathrm{h})\) is caused by the molecule–surface chemical interaction [37, 38]. The SERS signals of dopamine on \(\mathrm{D-MIL-125-NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\textrm{h})\) were further recorded against a concentration gradient ranging from \(6.6\times10^{-4}\) to \(6.6\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{M}\) (Fig. 2(c)), with the lowest detectable concentration at \(6.6~\times~10^{-7}\) M. For a comparison, the lowest detectable concentration of dopamine on the photo-excited \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) particle was \(6.6~\times~10^{-5}\) M (Fig. S7 in the ESM), which was two orders of magnitude higher than that of D-MIL- \(125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\mathrm{h})\) .
In addition to dopamine, a few other important probe
molecules such as bisphenol A (BPA, plasticizer), 4-TBC (a polymerization inhibitor), and 5-hydroxymethylfurfural (5-HMF, biotoxic substance) were tested on the D-MIL- \(125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) substrates, and similar results were obtained. For BPA and 5-HMF, a 10-fold increase in the SERS intensity of the \(1,176~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and a 6-fold increase in the SERS intensity of the \(1,618~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) were obtained on D-MIL- \(125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\textrm{h})\) at a concentration of \(8~\times~10^{-4}~\mathrm{~M},\) respectively, as compared with pristine \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH}_{2}\) (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)). For 4-TBC, the minimum LOD of \(\mathrm{D-MIL-125-NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\textrm{h})\) was \(6.6~\times~10^{-7}~\mathrm{~M},\) whereas almost no SERS signal was detectable on pristine \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH}_{2}\) even at a relatively large concentration of \(6.6\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~M~}\) (Fig. 3(c) and Fig. S8 in the ESM), indicating a discrepancy of three orders of magnitude in sensitivity between the two substrates. In addition to \(\mathrm{D-MIL-125-NH}_{2},\) similar enhanced SERS performances were also observed in other photoactivated MOF materials such as \(\mathrm{D-MIL-88-NH}_{2}\) and D\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Figs. 3(d)–3(f), and Figs. S9 and S10 in the ESM, vibration peak mode provided in the ESM). These results collectively suggest that the generation of oxygen vacancy defects by light irradiation is an effective strategy to improve the SERS signal of MOF materials.
More importantly, the \(\mathrm{PIV}_{0}\) defects in the MOFs exhibited excellent long-term stability, rendering them suitable for SERS applications, which is in significant contrast to the rapid elimination of \(\mathrm{PIV}_{0}\) defects in semiconductors, including those in the presence of metallic nanoparticles. First, the changes in the SERS intensity of photo-excited \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH}_{2},\) \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2},\) and Au\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) stored in the ambient atmosphere (temperature: \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) were investigated using Raman spectroscopy. In comparison to the intrinsic \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2},\) no further enhancement in SERS signals of BPA can be observed on the photo-excited \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) substrate upon air exposure for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) (Fig. S12 in the ESM), which is due to the rapid elimination of photo-induced oxygen vacancy defects in ambient atmosphere. Upon surface modification with Au nanoparticles, the SERS signal from the photo-induced \(\mathrm{Au-TiO}_{2}\)
remained for one hour longer compared with that of the unmodified sample (Figs. 4(a) and 4(b)); however, the additional time afforded was considered short.
Our results show that the intensity of the characteristic peak \((1,176~\mathrm{cm^{-1}})\) of BPA on D-MIL-125-NH2 (2 h) remained stable over a period of 60 days at \(25~^{\circ}\!C,\) with only approximately \(20\%\) attenuation compared with the initial intensity. Even after 120 days, the SERS enhancement on D-MIL-125-NH2 (2 h) remained superior to that on MIL-125-NH2, indicating that the partial maintenance of \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects had contributed to SERS activity in the defective MOFs (Figs. 4(c) and 4(d)). In addition to BPA, dopamine as a prototype probe molecule was tested on D-MIL-125-NH2 (2 h) substrates, where similar reproducibility in terms of SERS activity obtained was achieved during substrate storage (Fig. S13 in the ESM). Furthermore, the \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects in D-MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (2 h), after heating in the range of \(50{-}100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(15\ \mathrm{min}\) under ambient conditions, remained highly active during the SERS detection of BPA. In particular, as illustrated by the intensity of the characteristic peak \((1,176\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}})\) of BPA on D-MIL-125-NH2 \((2\,\,\mathrm{h})\) correlated with the temperature for the treatment of the substrate, the SERS activity of D-MIL-125-NH2 (2 h) remained stable at \(100\ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) where the initial intensity barely weakened. Above \(125\ \ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) the signal weakened significantly, with approximately \(90\%\) attenuation. For comparison, no apparent SERS enhancement was observed on the photo-excited \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -Au compared with the initial SERS performance of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -Au after heating to \(50\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C};\) this indicates that the SERS activity from photoinduced SERS enhancement is not available on the photo-excited \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -Au under this condition (Fig. S14 in the ESM). The results above all indicate that SERS behavior enabled by \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects in the MOFs offers a significant advantage over other semiconductors in terms of stability.
## 3 Mechanism
The significantly improved stability of the \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects in the MOFs can be explained based on the cluster-ligand framework topologies of the MOFs, which assumes the coexistence of steric
hindrance and electron delocalization around the oxygen vacancy defects. As illustrated in Fig. 5(a), the formation of photo-induced oxygen vacancies is initiated by charge carrier separation, where the holes are consumed by TEOA, leaving electrons at the Ti sites for the generation of \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) in the Ti-oxo clusters. Oxygen vacancies may be generated around the reductive \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) sites by the breakage of Ti–O bonds, as already discovered experimentally and theoretically in varied MOFs [21]. As suggested by the results of density functional theory (DFT) calculations, the breakage of Ti–O bonds between the reductive \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) sites and the 2- aminoterephthalic ligand may be favored in the photo-excited D-MIL-125-NH2, based on its relatively low system energy \(\left(E\ \ =\ \ -1,881.69\ \ \mathrm{eV}\right)\) when compared with other possible configurations (Fig. S15 in the ESM). However, coordination between –O– dangling groups in the ligand and the adjacent metal center \(\left(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\right)\) may exist [21], which can exert electronegative surroundings that prevent the access of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) upon air exposure. Such steric hindrance, typically absent in semiconducting oxides (e.g., \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)), contributes to the stabilization of \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects in the MOFs. On the other hand, electron delocalization around the oxygen vacancy defects in the MOFs also has a vital impact on the stability of vacancy defects, which can be revealed by the calculated amount of charges carried by the Ti atoms adjacent to the \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects (Fig. 5(b) and Fig. S16 in the ESM). When oxygen vacancies are introduced into \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), the charges at neighboring Ti atoms are reduced significantly (from \(2.25\;\mathrm{e}\) to \(1.01\;\mathrm{e}\) for Ti1 site, from \(2.25\,\mathrm{e}\) to \(1.77\,\mathrm{e}\) for Ti2 site), suggesting the markedly reduced stability of the defective state, which is responsible for the rapid healing of oxygen vacancies in \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) upon air exposure. By contrast, charges were altered slightly at the Ti atom sites (from \(2.07\;\mathrm{e}\) to \(1.97\,\mathrm{e}\) for Ti1 site, from \(2.14\;\mathrm{e}\) to \(2.11\;\mathrm{e}\) for Ti2 site, and from \(1.95\,\mathrm{e}\) to \(1.87\,\mathrm{e}\) for Ti3 site, respectively) when a similar oxygen-vacancy was introduction for the modeling structure of D-MIL-125-NH2; this clearly demonstrates the charge delocalization in the defective structure, which contributed to the improved stability of \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects in the MOFs.
The \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects are vital to the enhancement in the SERS defect based on charge-transfer coupling of the substrate–analyte
system, as illustrated by the altered charge-transfer pathways when dopamine was combined with \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{D-MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\). First, the strong interaction between dopamine and D-MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) can be directly observed via UV–Vis spectroscopy, as indicated by an emerging absorption band at \(500{-}600\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. S17 in the ESM). The new absorption band, which is not observed in the \(\mathrm{MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) –dopamine system, indicates the formation of charge-transfer complexes enabled by defects in the D-MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) –dopamine system; this is a prerequisite for interfacial charge-transfer transitions that is crucial for achieving enhanced SERS activity. Subsequently, by combining the results from UV–Vis absorption, electrochemical Mott–Schottky analysis, and XPS valence band spectra (Bigs. S18 and S19), the valence band maximum (VBM) and conduction band minimum (CBM) for \(\mathrm{MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{D-MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) can be obtained, as demonstrated by the locations with respect to the molecule levels (lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) and highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO)) of dopamine (Fig. 5(c)). Owing to the presence of photo-induced defects, an upshifted VBM in addition to a slightly narrowed energy band \(E_{\mathrm{g}}\) \((2.62\;\mathrm{eV})\) can be obtained in \(\mathrm{D-MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\textrm{h})\) as compared with pristine \(\mathrm{MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) \((E_{\mathrm{g}}\,=\,2.70\,\mathrm{\eV})\). The altered energy-band structure of \(\mathrm{D-MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\textrm{h})\) by photo-induced defects resulted in an improved band-level matching between the MOF substrate and the dopamine analyte, as indicated by the approximate locations of the CBM \((-6.28\ \mathrm{\eV})\) and HOMO
\((-5.95\,\mathrm{\eV})\), thereby facilitating the ground-state charge transfer transitions \((\mu_{\mathrm{GSCT}})\) between \(\mathrm{D-MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) \((2\ \textrm{h})\) and the chemisorbed dopamine molecules. Furthermore, a photo-induced charge transfer transition \((\mu_{\mathrm{PICT}})\) occurring at an energy of \(2.29\;\mathrm{eV}\), which is in close resonance with the incident \(532{\mathrm{-nm}}\) laser \((\lambda_{\mathrm{L}}=2.33\ \mathrm{eV})\), can be expected from the HOMO of dopamine to the CBM of \(\mathrm{D-MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\textrm{h})\). Notably, vibronic coupling among the aforementioned computed tomography (CT) transitions (including \(\mu_{\mathrm{GSCT}}\) and \(\mu_{\mathrm{PICT}}\)) through a Herzberg–Teller coupling term[39] may contribute to the significantly improved SERS enhancement in \(\mathrm{D-MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) \((2\,\textrm{h})\) compared with pristine \(\mathrm{MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\), which possessed a different energy band structure.
Moreover, the band-level matching between the MOFs and analytes may be insufficient to adequately explain the enhanced SERS activity, whereas improved charge-transfer ability within MOF substrate enabled by photo-induced defects would give a further contribution. To clarify the altered charge transfer behaviors of \(\mathrm{D-MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) with respect to pristine \(\mathrm{MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\), the intervalence charge transfer band revealed by the UV–Vis diffuse reflectance spectrum was considered. New absorption bands were clearly visible in the range of \(1.55{-}2.50\;\mathrm{eV}\) for D-MIL- \(125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\), where an increase in signal intensity with prolonged reaction time was indicated (Fig. 5(d)). The emerging absorptions in \(\mathrm{D-MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) are attributable to a series of intervalence charge transfer bands, which suggest mid-gap polarons and
possibly enhanced charge transport properties [22], otherwise absent in pristine MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). Accordingly, benefiting from the stabilized photo-induced vacancy defects, the vibronic coupling of multiple CT transitions \((\mu_{\mathrm{GSC}}\) and \(\mu_{\mathrm{PICT}})\) and enhanced charge transfer within MOFs can be realized simultaneously, which is responsible for the significantly enhanced SERS activity observed in the D-MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) substrate.
## 4 Discussion
In summary, the photo-induced vacancy defects can be stabilized by the photo-excitation of MOF materials, which is an ideal material for evaluating their contributions to SERS performance. For instance, \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects can be maintained in D-MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) for more than 2 weeks under ambient atmosphere, owing to stabilization via steric hindrance and charge delocalization around vacancy defects, as afforded by the cluster-ligand framework topologies of MOFs. With the formation of stable \(\mathrm{PIV}_{\mathrm{O}}\) defects, prominent SERS enhancement surpassing that of pristine MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was achieved in D-MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), as evident from the detection of various analyte molecules, and the transcendence in SERS activity remained distinguishable even after 120 days of storage. Furthermore, the vibronic coupling of multiple CT transitions and enhanced charge transfer within MOFs contributed to the significantly enhanced SERS activity observed on the D-MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) substrate. More importantly, this strategy of photo-inducing stabilized oxygen vacancy defects in MOFs is widely applicable for achieving enhanced SERS performance in MOF-based substrates.
